      minWords: 250, use: ["fall flat", "punchline", "banter", "in stitches"],
      model: "Lost in Translation: Why My Best Joke Died in London\n\nWhen I moved to London for my master's degree, I assumed that being funny would help me make friends. At home I had a reputation as the person who could keep a whole table in stitches. Within a week, I discovered that humour does not always travel well.\n\nMy first mistake happened at a welcome party. Someone asked where I was from, and I replied with a joke that always worked in Moscow, a long story about a bureaucrat, a queue and a missing stamp. I delivered it perfectly. I paused before the punchline. Nobody laughed. One girl nodded politely and asked whether that had really happened to me.\n\nIt took me a while to understand why the joke had fallen flat. Part of the problem was context: my audience had never stood in a Soviet-style queue, so the absurdity meant nothing to them. But the bigger issue was style. British humour, I soon realised, is often understated and self-mocking. People make fun of themselves, the weather or their own failures, rather than telling long, structured jokes. My story sounded less like banter and more like a performance.\n\nGradually I adapted. I started listening more than I talked, noticing what made my classmates laugh. I learnt to make small, ironic comments instead of telling anecdotes, and to laugh at my own mistakes with the language. Ironically, those mistakes became my most successful material.\n\nMy advice to anyone moving abroad is simple. Don't panic if your jokes fall flat at first; it happens to everyone. Watch, listen and be ready to laugh at yourself. Humour is one of the last things we learn in a new culture, but it is also one of the most rewarding."
