      model: "Should Language Learners Bother with Slang?\n\nAsk most language teachers about slang and they will tell you to concentrate on the basics first. Ask most learners who have moved abroad, and they will tell you that the basics did not prepare them for their first conversation in a pub. Both sides have a point.\n\nThe case for learning slang is mainly about understanding. Native speakers, especially younger ones, use informal and colloquial language constantly. If you only know textbook English, you may follow a news broadcast perfectly but get lost in a group chat or a sitcom. Recognising words like sus, mid or no cap helps you understand what is actually being said, including the jokes, which are often the first thing a learner misses.\n\nThere is also a social argument. Slang signals belonging. Knowing a few current expressions can make you seem relaxed and approachable rather than stiff and formal.\n\nHowever, the risks are real. Slang changes quickly, and a word that sounded fresh two years ago may now be considered cringe. A learner who uses outdated expressions can sound out of touch, or even ridiculous, particularly if their accent and grammar are otherwise formal. Slang is also tied to age, region and social group. An expression that works among teenagers in London may sound bizarre coming from a forty-year-old accountant in Manchester.\n\nIn my view, the answer lies in the difference between recognising and using. Learners should certainly try to understand common slang, because it helps them follow real conversations. Using it, however, is best left until they are confident about who says it, when and why. Until then, a curious question such as 'Sorry, what does that mean?' is far more useful than a badly chosen word."
