      text: "Samuel Johnson is said to have called punning the lowest form of wit. Oscar Wilde, never one to let a good remark go unimproved, supposedly added: 'and therefore the foundation of all wit'. Whoever said what, the argument has never been settled. Some people treat wordplay as an art form; others respond to every pun with a theatrical groan. Interestingly, the groan may be the point.\n\nConsider the humble dad joke. 'I'm reading a book on anti-gravity. It's impossible to put down.' Nobody laughs at this the way they laugh at a brilliant comedian. Instead they roll their eyes, sigh, and perhaps repeat it to someone else later that day. Psychologists who study humour suggest that the groan is a kind of social game. The teller pretends to be proud of something terrible; the listener pretends to be offended. Both enjoy the performance, and the pun becomes an excuse for a small moment of shared silliness.\n\nEnglish is unusually rich territory for this sort of game. Centuries of borrowing from Latin, French, Norse and dozens of other languages have left it full of homophones and words with multiple meanings. 'Interest' can mean curiosity or money; 'bear' can be an animal, a verb or a falling stock market. The same chaotic history has produced spellings that bear little relation to pronunciation, which gives punsters endless material.\n\nWordplay is not only about puns, of course. British newspapers have a long tradition of headlines built on double meanings. When a small Scottish football team, Caledonian Thistle, beat Celtic, one of the most famous clubs in the country, a tabloid ran the headline 'Super Caley Go Ballistic, Celtic Are Atrocious', a play on the song from Mary Poppins. Journalists still quote it as the gold standard of the form. At the other end of the market, cryptic crosswords, in which every clue is a small linguistic puzzle combining a definition with anagrams, hidden words and homophones, are taken extremely seriously by millions of solvers.\n\nThen there are the accidental forms. A malapropism occurs when someone confuses two similar-sounding words, as in 'He's the pineapple of politeness' instead of 'pinnacle'. A spoonerism swaps the first sounds of two words; the Reverend William Spooner, after whom it is named, supposedly told a student that he had 'tasted the whole worm' rather than 'wasted the whole term'. A mondegreen is a misheard lyric, and nearly everyone has one, usually discovered with horror while singing loudly in front of other people.\n\nWhat all these forms share is a delight in the gap between what language says and what it might have said. Wordplay reminds us that words are slippery, that meaning depends on context, and that a single sound can open two doors at once. For a language learner, this can be infuriating. It can also be a sign of real progress. The moment you groan at an English pun, rather than looking it up, you have understood something no textbook can easily teach.",
