      minWords: 260, use: ["play on words", "double entendre", "groaner", "lost on"],
      model: "Review: Pun Intended at the Edinburgh Fringe\n\nIf you have ever groaned at a dad joke and then secretly repeated it to a colleague, Pun Intended is the show for you. Over sixty breathless minutes, comedian Dev Sharma attempts to set a personal record for the number of puns told on stage, and the result is exhausting, ridiculous and frequently brilliant.\n\nThe format is simple. Sharma tells the story of a disastrous family wedding, but almost every sentence contains a play on words. The caterer couldn't cut the mustard, the band fell flat in more than one sense, and the best man's speech was a toast that burned. Some lines are genuine gems; others are shameless groaners, delivered with such obvious pride that the audience's suffering becomes part of the joke.\n\nSharma is particularly good at the double entendre. A long sequence about his uncle's impressive organ, which turns out to be a church instrument, had the room in hysterics, though it is probably not one for younger viewers. He also plays cleverly with British and American differences, including an excellent routine about ordering chips on both sides of the Atlantic.\n\nNot everything works. Towards the end the pace becomes relentless, and a section on cryptic crosswords will be lost on anyone who has never attempted one. A little more variety would help the show breathe.\n\nFor language learners, Pun Intended is both a challenge and a gift. You will miss some of the jokes, especially those that depend on regional slang. But the ones you catch will make you feel, for a moment, like a native speaker, which is perhaps the best compliment any comedian could receive.\n\nVerdict: four stars, and at least forty groans."
