    title: "Понять шутку",
    text: "When I arrived in Dublin twelve years ago, I had a degree in English, a certificate proving I was 'proficient', and absolutely no idea what anyone was saying. My first landlord told me the flat was 'grand', and I spent a week waiting for something impressive to happen. In Ireland, I eventually learnt, 'grand' means 'acceptable', or sometimes 'not great, but let's not talk about it'.\n\nThat was the first of many lessons. Textbooks prepare you for the language people ought to speak. They rarely prepare you for the language they actually use: the clipped sentences, the idioms, the slang, the jokes that depend on a television programme everyone watched in 1994. In my first year, I understood almost every word at work and almost nothing at the pub.\n\nIdioms were the first hurdle. I had memorised dozens of them, which turned out to be a mixed blessing. Native speakers do use idioms, but they rarely use them in the tidy dictionary form I had learnt. A colleague would say 'Well, that's the cat well and truly out of the bag', or 'Let's not throw the baby out with the bathwater, but maybe give the bath a rinse'. They bent, extended and mocked idioms constantly, and I could only follow when I knew the original well enough to notice the twist.\n\nHumour was harder still. Irish and British humour depend heavily on understatement and irony, which are almost invisible to an outsider. When my manager described a client meeting that had collapsed into shouting as 'a bit lively', I took her at face value and asked whether we should schedule another one. The silence was memorable. Later I discovered that the national sport is not football or rugby but teasing, and that being teased is a sign of acceptance. The first time a colleague mocked my accent, I was offended. The first time nobody did for a week, I was worried.\n\nRegional variation added another layer. My Dublin colleagues said 'I'm after finishing it' to mean they had just finished something; a visitor from London thought they were about to start. Americans on conference calls had 'gotten' things done while the British had 'got' them done, and nobody could agree on whether the team 'is' or 'are'. At first this felt like chaos. Gradually I realised it was simply what a living language looks like.\n\nThe turning point came, oddly enough, with a pun. A colleague was complaining that the office printer kept jamming, and I said, without thinking, that it was clearly 'under a lot of pressure'. There was a pause, then a groan, then laughter. Someone said, 'She's one of us now.' It was a terrible joke. It was also, I think, the proudest moment of my professional life.\n\nMy advice to anyone preparing to move abroad is not to stop studying grammar. But spend at least as much time listening to how people play with the language as to how they use it correctly. Correct English will get you a job. Playful English will get you friends.",
