    minWords: 300, use: ["idiom", "slang", "pun", "regional"],
    model: "Can You Truly Master a Language Without Its Humour?\n\nMost language exams test grammar, vocabulary and the ability to write a well-organised essay. Very few test whether you can tell when someone is joking. Yet anyone who has lived abroad knows that understanding humour is often the last and most difficult stage of learning a language. I would argue that without it, mastery remains incomplete.\n\nThe first reason is that humour is woven into everyday communication. Native speakers constantly use idioms, often in altered or ironic forms. A colleague who says that the cat is out of the bag, and possibly the bag is out of the window, assumes you know the original idiom well enough to enjoy the twist. If you do not, you miss not just a joke but part of the meaning.\n\nSlang creates a similar barrier. Informal expressions change quickly and vary between age groups and countries. A learner who understands formal English perfectly may still struggle with a group of friends whose conversation is full of current slang. Being able to follow, and occasionally share, that playful register is part of genuinely belonging.\n\nWordplay is perhaps the clearest test. A pun only works if you hold two meanings of a word in your mind at once, instantly and without effort. That kind of automatic understanding is exactly what we mean by fluency. Regional variation adds a further layer: a joke that works in London may fall flat in New York, and a learner needs to recognise these differences to avoid confusion or offence.\n\nOn the other hand, it would be unfair to say that someone who misses jokes cannot use a language effectively. Many professionals work successfully in a second language for years without understanding every pun. Communication, in the practical sense, does not depend on humour.\n\nNevertheless, mastery is more than effective communication. It means feeling at home in a language and being able to play with it as native speakers do. In that sense, humour is not an optional extra but the final proof that a language has become your own."
